     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg per day. The usual maximum for adults is 2,550 mg per day (2,000 mg for extended-release), usually split into doses of 500\u20131,000 mg. At twice the maximum, the patient would face a serious risk of lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function. The number may be a typo. For example, 850 mg three times daily is 2,550 mg per day, and 1,700 mg per day would be 850 mg twice daily.\n\n2. **A prescriber needs to place the order.** You've said you're not a clinician. Medication orders should come from a licensed prescriber, or be confirmed by one, so that someone with clinical responsibility for the patient has reviewed them.\n\nTo move this forward, the patient's treating prescriber can confirm the intended dose and frequency. If they give me a dose within the standard range, I'll enter it right away. For example, they could specify \"metformin 850 mg PO TID with meals\" or \"500 mg PO BID with meals.\""
      }
